&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a performance of the play &amp;quot;Lost Stories&amp;quot; by &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mikhail-zygar/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mikhail Zygar&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. Mikhail Zygar will be joined in this performance by director and actor &lt;strong&gt;Whitney White&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-lipovetsky/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Lipovetsky&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; will moderate the post-performance Q&amp;amp;A session.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The play &amp;quot;Lost Stories&amp;quot; by Mikhail Zygar explores how ordinary people can change the course of history, and how decisions made by ordinary individuals ultimately led to the collapse of the Soviet Union. The play is based on the book &amp;quot;The Dark Side of the Earth,&amp;quot; published last November by Scribner, which examines the final 30 years of the Soviet Union&amp;mdash;how Soviet society evolved and how the Soviet people ultimately defeated the Soviet system&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Can Europe Act? Power and Sovereignty in a Fragmenting World&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A conversation with&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://worldleaders.columbia.edu/directory/olaf-scholz" target="_blank"&gt;Olaf Scholz&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Former Chancellor of the Federal Republic of Germany&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;and&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://europe.columbia.edu/content/adam-tooze" target="_blank"&gt;Adam Tooze&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Kathryn and Shelby Cullom Davis Professor of History, Director of the&amp;nbsp;European Institute, and&amp;nbsp;Chair of the&amp;nbsp;Committee on Global Thought&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As geopolitical tensions rise and the global order becomes increasingly unstable, Europe faces a defining challenge: can it act strategically and assert its role on the world stage?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this conversation, former German Chancellor Olaf Scholz reflects on Europe&amp;rsquo;s capacity to shape its own future &amp;ndash; from security and economic resilience to political leadership in times of crisis.&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Columbia University students are invited to participate in a moderated question-and-answer session.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This program is cosponsored by the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://europe.columbia.edu/"&gt;European Institute&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registration is now closed. You can tune in for the livestream on the &lt;a href="https://worldleaders.columbia.edu/live"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;World Leaders Forum&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; website.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The student teams in SIPA&amp;rsquo;s&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Workshop in Sustainable Development Practice&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;a href="https://www.sipa.columbia.edu/sipa-education/capstone-workshops/info-dg-students" target="_blank"&gt;the EPD/D&amp;amp;G Workshop&lt;/a&gt;) have been working this year on cutting-edge projects with 17 clients in 15 countries.&amp;nbsp; Notably, three of the projects involve SIPA alumni as clients, and six faculty advisors (as well as the workshop director) are alumni.&amp;nbsp; This year&amp;rsquo;s projects support AI policy and AI innovations in education and small business development, climate adaptation in rural and urban areas, gender in agriculture and financial inclusion, nutrition and care policy innovations, impact monitoring of data centers and extractive industries, localization of humanitarian assistance, and curbing online scams. &amp;nbsp;On April 30 and May 1, the teams will present highlights of their work in a hybrid conference hosted by SIPA.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Workshop clients, faculty advisors, members of the Columbia/SIPA community, SIPA alumni, and the general public are warmly invited and encouraged to join any sessions of interest.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This event will be hosted in person (for CUID holders &amp;amp; guests) and online. Registration is required for non-CUID holders to receive a QR code and for remote attendees to receive a Zoom link.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This year&amp;#39;s workshop clients&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;include AlphaMundi Foundation, Brava Foundation, Climate Resilience for All/HERA, Elea Data Centers, Enabling Qapital, Fundaci&amp;oacute;n Ronda, International Trade Centre, Kumulus Water, Ministry of AI and Digital Development of Kazakhstan, One Acre Fund, Pencils of Promise, Rights CoLab, Save the Children Jordan, United Nations Development Programme&amp;rsquo;s Crisis Bureau and Digital, AI and Innovation Hub, UN Resident Coordinator&amp;rsquo;s Office in Mongolia, and World Food Programme&amp;rsquo;s Regional Centre of Excellence against Hunger and Malnutrition.&lt;/p&gt;
