&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Xiaotian Zhang, PhD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Assistant Professor&lt;br /&gt;
Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology&lt;br /&gt;
The University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title&lt;/strong&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&amp;quot;Mechanism and therapy of NPM1 mutated leukemia&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Mutations of NPM1 is the most common mutations in acute myeloid leukemia. The mutation leads to the substantial alterations in the mutant NPM1&amp;#39;s subcellular location from nucleolus to cytoplasm and gene expression patterns. We have shown mutant NPM1 act as a neomorphic transcriptional amplifier in controlling HOX/MEIS1 gene expression. In the talk, the novel mechanism of NPM1 condensate formation will be discussed. The efficacy of Menin inhibitor in NPM1c AML will also be discussed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Hosted by Dr. Chao Lu.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join University Life&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;Legacy of New York&lt;/strong&gt; series for a guided food tour through the vibrant Lower East Side (LES).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;On this immersive walking experience, we&amp;rsquo;ll explore the neighborhood&amp;rsquo;s history through curated tastings at cherished local markets and shops, tracing the immigrant stories that have shaped the LES. From the historic Jewish East Side to Little Italy and Chinatown, the LES has long served as a first home for generations of immigrants to New York City.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As we walk, you&amp;rsquo;ll sample small bites that reflect the enduring influence of Dominican, Puerto Rican, Jewish, Italian, and Chinese communities, and experience firsthand how food continues to play an important role in connecting people to their heritage and culture.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Come hungry and ready to explore one of New York City&amp;rsquo;s most dynamic neighborhoods through its flavors, stories, and community.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Legacy of New York&lt;/strong&gt; series invites students to explore the social movements, histories, and communities that have shaped New York City. Through museum visits, walking tours, and site-based learning experiences, this series seeks to highlight and celebrate the diversity of communities, cultures, and events.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
